{"birds": [{"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 20, "first": "8:01 AM", "last": "9:54 AM", "ts": 1794747717, "mins": [481, 482, 501, 502, 505, 506, 506, 507, 514, 515, 515, 515, 516, 517, 517, 548, 548, 549, 561, 594], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png?v=1793990894", "w": 484, "h": 472, "len": 557.0, "v": 1793990894, "beak": [0.021, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "D4AAAH/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAA/4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAH/8AAB//gAAf/+AAH//wAA//+AAH//wAB//+AAP//gAA//8AAH//gAA//+AAHZ/wAB2P8AA5h+AAcwP4f/uA+P//+Dj////4////+P////gD///4AAD/+AAAAPg=="}}, {"name": "American Crow", "sci": "Corvus brachyrhynchos", "n": 14, "first": "8:02 AM", "last": "12:01 PM", "ts": 1794747733, "mins": [482, 483, 486, 486, 508, 518, 521, 552, 552, 553, 560, 719, 719, 721], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Crows remember individual human faces for years, and they seem to pass the grudge along to other crows.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793112990", "snd": "sounds/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793113335", "sndby": "David Vander Pluym, xeno-canto XC289011, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "crow", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/crow.png?v=1793031428", "w": 553, "h": 364, "len": 568.0, "v": 1793031428, "beak": [0.94, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 21, "b": "AAAHwAAAD/gAAB/+AAB//gAD/+AAD//AAB//wAB//4AB//+AB///AAf//wAP//4AH//4AH//8AD//8AA/x+AAPgPwAbwF/6/f////3///7Bhw5AA"}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 4, "first": "8:03 AM", "last": "9:55 AM", "ts": 1794747790, "mins": [483, 525, 579, 595], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png?v=1793990893", "w": 649, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "v": 1793990893, "beak": [0.851, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 30, "b": "AAA/AAAA/4AAAP/wAAH/4AAD/4AAA/+AAAP/gAAH/4AAD/+AAA//gAAf/4AAP/+AAD//gAB//4AA//8AAf/+AgH//g4D//w8A//48Af/98AP//8AH8b8AB8P8AAcPzAAOfgwAHPgAADvgAAA3gAAAHgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 67, "first": "8:09 AM", "last": "12:34 PM", "ts": 1794748146, "mins": [489, 492, 500, 500, 503, 503, 503, 505, 508, 509, 518, 519, 520, 522, 523, 523, 524, 524, 525, 525, 526, 526, 527, 528, 528, 529, 529, 530, 530, 532, 532, 533, 533, 534, 534, 534, 535, 536, 538, 539, 539, 564, 565, 566, 569, 579, 580, 583, 583, 584, 588, 596, 596, 598, 599, 608, 655, 657, 657, 658, 659, 677, 686, 694, 737, 738, 754], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png?v=1793046043", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 21, "first": "8:25 AM", "last": "12:04 PM", "ts": 1794749155, "mins": [505, 506, 506, 507, 507, 540, 540, 541, 541, 541, 542, 542, 545, 545, 562, 603, 603, 616, 616, 617, 724], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png?v=1793045350", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "v": 1793045350, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 21, "first": "8:54 AM", "last": "11:47 AM", "ts": 1794750854, "mins": [534, 536, 536, 537, 548, 549, 549, 550, 550, 550, 551, 552, 553, 556, 592, 592, 604, 666, 682, 706, 707], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail feathers flash as they flit away.", "vox": "sounds/voice/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113009", "snd": "sounds/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113154", "sndby": "Phoenix Birder, xeno-canto XC563632, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "junco", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/junco.png?v=1793991313", "w": 534, "h": 447, "len": 625.0, "v": 1793991313, "beak": [0.022, 0.14, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 27, "b": "D4AAAD/AAAA/4AAA//AAAP/wAAA//AAAH/4AAD//AAA//4AAP//AAD//4AA///AAH//4wB///cAP//+AB///AAf//wAB//+AAP//wAA//8AAH+PgAD8B8AH+AHgD/gA8D+AAHg/gAA8AAAAH"}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 6, "first": "9:15 AM", "last": "12:11 PM", "ts": 1794752148, "mins": [555, 567, 608, 616, 616, 731], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png?v=1793031430", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "v": 1793031430, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 2, "first": "9:22 AM", "last": "11:07 AM", "ts": 1794752528, "mins": [562, 667], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png?v=1793031431", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "v": 1793031431, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 37, "first": "9:22 AM", "last": "12:14 PM", "ts": 1794752560, "mins": [562, 578, 581, 584, 587, 587, 588, 590, 592, 595, 596, 596, 597, 597, 598, 598, 599, 610, 620, 638, 638, 669, 672, 674, 674, 674, 675, 677, 691, 691, 692, 694, 719, 719, 723, 730, 734], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png?v=1793046043", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 3, "first": "9:29 AM", "last": "9:30 AM", "ts": 1794752990, "mins": [569, 570, 570], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png?v=1793031427", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "v": 1793031427, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 1, "first": "9:35 AM", "last": "9:35 AM", "ts": 1794753352, "mins": [575], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png?v=1793990895", "w": 451, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "v": 1793990895, "beak": [0.206, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "ADwAAAD+AAAB/wAAA/8AAAP/gAAA/8AAAH/AAAD/8AAA//AAA